		(property "MPN" "1N4148W"
			(at 97.79 60.96 0)
			(hide yes)
			(show_name no)
			(do_not_autoplace no)
			(effects
				(font
					(size 1.27 1.27)
				)
			)
		)
		(property "Note" "Silicon, NOT Schottky - identical part to D5 and for the same reason. D2 blocks reverse current into BT1, which is a PRIMARY cell with no charge path. Schottky reverse leakage is orders of magnitude higher and roughly doubles per 10C, so a hot parked cabin would trickle-charge a non-rechargeable cell. Do NOT 'optimize' to a Schottky for the lower Vf: the ~0.4-0.5V drop at the ~20uA backup load is deliberate and accepted. The 2-pin package is deliberate too - SOD-123 cannot physically accept the BAT54A/C/S common-node dual that PCBWay proposed on quote T-B4W341056A (2026-08-14), so that substitution is now impossible rather than merely forbidden. Was a BAT54 in SOT-23 until 2026-08-15. UNVERIFIED: the MAX-M10M V_BCKP minimum has not been checked against u-blox's integration manual (datasheets/ holds only the 2-page product summary, which does not state it). Silicon leaves ~1.55V at cell EOL and cold raises Vf further; confirm the floor before trusting backup hold time at the tail of the cell."
			(at 97.79 60.96 0)
			(hide yes)
			(show_name no)
			(do_not_autoplace no)
			(effects
				(font
					(size 1.27 1.27)
				)
			)
		)
		(property "Quote" "PCBWay T-B4W341056A (5 units, 2026-08-14): SUPERSEDED. That quote priced D2 as a BAT54 (Schottky, SOT-23) at $0.037 ea; the part was replaced 2026-08-15. D2 is now the same 1N4148W / SOD-123 as D5 and must be re-quoted - tell PCBWay to merge the two into one line item."
			(at 97.79 60.96 0)
			(hide yes)
			(show_name no)
			(do_not_autoplace no)
			(effects
				(font
					(size 1.27 1.27)
				)
			)
		)
